   "B": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the family court act and the social services law, in relation to removing special powers granted to the society for the prevention of cruelty to children; and repealing certain provisions of the criminal procedure law, the public officers law and the social services law relating thereto; and providing for the repeal of certain provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "A7846",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A7846-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1012, 1024, 1034 & 119, Fam Ct Act; amd §§417, 386 & 372, rpld §423 sub 1 ¶(e), Soc Serv L; rpld & add §2.10 sub 7-a, CP L; rpld & add §3 sub 35, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Family Court Act",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A7846B\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend the family court act and the social services law, in\nrelation to removing special powers granted to the society for the\nprevention of cruelty to children; and repealing certain provisions of\nthe criminal procedure law, the public officers law and the social\nservices law relating thereto; and providing for the repeal of certain\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nRepeals special powers granted to the Society for the Prevention of\nCruelty to Children (SPCC), which are in direct conflict with the\nauthority of state and local child protective services\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n1) Amends subdivision (i) of section 1012 of the family court act, by\nremoving SPCCs from the definition as a child protective agency;\n\n2) Amends subdivision (a) of section 1024 of the family court act, by\nremoving SPCCs authority to take a child into protective custody;\n\n3) Amends subdivision I of section 1034 of the family court act, by\nremoving SPCCs authority to conduct child abuse investigations;\n\n4) Amends paragraph (a) of subdivision I of section 417 of the social\nservices law, by removing SPCCs authority to take a child into protec-\ntive custody;\n\n5) Amends subdivision 2 of section 386 of the social services law, by\nremoving SPCC's authority to visit group homes at its discretion;\n\n6) Amends subdivision 5 of section 372 of the social services law by\neliminating references to the SPCC;\n\n7) Repeals subdivision 7-a of section 2.10 of the criminal procedure law\nby removing SPCC members authorization as peace officers;\n\n8) Adds a new subdivision 7-a to section 2.10 of the criminal procedure\nlaw to authorize officers or agents of an SPCC in Rockland County to\nobtain peace officer status;\n\n9) Removes the ability of SPCC in NYC to designate its members as peace\nofficers;\n\n10) Authorizes officers or agents of any SPCC in Rockland County to\nobtain peace officer status;\n\n11) Removes the SPCC as being considered a duly authorized association\nwithin which children may be placed;\n\n12) Removes SPCC's authority to investigate reports of child abuse.\n\n13) Exempts Erie County from sections 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 11 and 12 of\nthis Act for a period of five years from the effective date, upon which\ntime the legislature shall review the fiscal circumstances which neces-\nsitated such exemption.\n\n14) Sets an immediate effective date, provide that sections 8, 10 and\n\n13 expire five years after the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nSocieties for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children (SPCC) were first\nestablished in New York State in the mid-1870s, in response to a need\nfor protecting children from abuse and neglect. At that time, there were\nno state laws or authorities to protect children from abusive situ-\nations. For over a century these organizations helped to protect the\nchildren of our state. However, with the enactment of the Child Protec-\ntive Services Act in 1973, the state assumed the role of protecting\nchildren against abuse and neglect.\n\nIn July of 2001, the New York State Commission of Investigation released\na report that details many abuses of authority still committed by agents\nof the SPCCs. On numerous occasions, it has been documented that the\nagents of the SPCCs have misrepresented themselves as law enforcement\nofficers by wearing uniforms, carrying shields, and using parking plac-\nards, which falsely identified them as state law enforcement. These\nagents have also been reported to have interfered in police investi-\ngations, and to have asked school officials and hospitals to contact\nthem if they identify any cases of abuse rather than informing the State\nhotline.\n\nDespite these findings, SPCCs are still authorized to provide child\nprotective services, and without any State oversight or regulation.  The\nSPCCs played a very valuable role for the children of New York State for\nmore than a hundred years, and many of the services they provide to the\ncommunity are still very valuable and necessary.  However, there is no\nlonger a place for the SPCCs to provide child protective services,\nunless they have contracted with a local social services district like\nany other agency. The laws put in place over a hundred years ago are no\nlonger necessary, and if anything, are dangerous to the system which has\nbeen established by the State to ensure the safety of all the children\nin New York State.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2008: A.10516-A/S.3153\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately, provided that sections 8, 10 and 13 shall expire five years\nafter the effective date of this act.\n\n",
